[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: web_server_1
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: Application layer (Apache on web_server_1) – HTTPS/TLS virtual host and listener are not configured.
Mechanism: The user’s bookmark hits https://web1.local. Because Apache on web_server_1 only listens on port 80 (HTTP) and does not have a TLS listener on 443, the TCP connection for HTTPS cannot be established, so browsers report a hang/timeout. Plain HTTP works, which is why http://web1.local loads instantly.
Localization: On web_server_1 (10.200.0.4), netstat shows only 0.0.0.0:80 LISTEN and no 443 listener. From host_1_1_1_1: HTTP to http://web1.local returns 200 OK (Apache/2.4.62) with fast times; HTTPS to https://web1.local fails (curl http_code=000). DNS resolves web1.local to 10.200.0.4 and network reachability (ICMP) is fine.
Root cause: web1.local is configured as HTTP-only; there is no HTTPS virtual host or certificate, while the user’s bookmark requests HTTPS.
Corrective fix: Either (a) advise users to use http://web1.local, or (b) enable HTTPS on web_server_1. To enable HTTPS: install/enable SSL in Apache (a2enmod ssl), create/enable a 443 vhost (a2ensite default-ssl or a proper web1.local-ssl.conf), install a certificate (self-signed or internal CA), and reload Apache (systemctl reload apache2). Optionally, configure the 443 vhost to serve the same content or redirect to HTTP if HTTPS is not required. After enabling 443, https://web1.local will load normally. No routing or server “crash” was observed. === END ===